#!/bin/sh
# script/crate-move: stage a crate's move to a repository of its own, as a dry run.
#
#     script/crate-move --dry-run --into <empty dir> <crate>
#     script/crate-move --dry-run --into <dir> --no-build <crate>        # the static half, seconds
#     script/crate-move --dry-run --into <dir> --template <checkout> --template-rev v7 <crate>
#
# Name: provisional, minted 2026-11-05 (UTC) for milestone 1034 (a runbook for moving a crate out
# of nife), a maintainer decision. Named for the step of notes/crate-move.md it stands in for.
#
# **Why this exists.** `script/crate-standalone` asks whether a crate would stand alone and builds
# a throwaway copy. A move needs more than that answer: the crate repository's first tree, with the
# shared template's scaffold and its callers pinned, the `Name:` blocks deleted (git history in this
# tree is their only record), and the falsification patches rewritten for `-p1`. The pilot's move
# did those by hand and spent its first CI runs finding what it missed. This builds that tree
# locally and runs the template's own cheap checks on it, so the findings arrive before a repository
# exists.
#
# **What it does**, in order:
#
# 1. Fetches the template, `nifeos/crate-workflows`, at its newest `v*` release (or
#    `--template-rev`), by a fresh clone into a temporary directory, or from `--template`, a local
#    checkout it only reads (`git archive`).
# 2. Copies the crate's tracked files into `--into`, which must be empty or absent.
# 3. Adds the template's `scaffold/`: the callers go to `.github/workflows/` with `@PINS@` replaced
#    by the release's commit, `ci.yml` gets the crate's targets (docs.rs `targets`, else the three
#    bare-metal ones) and `allow-unpublished: true` for the soak. A file the crate already carries
#    wins over the scaffold's, and says so.
# 4. Deletes every `Name:` block (`crate_release.strip_name_blocks`), regenerates each falsification
#    patch over the stripped source, and sets `repository` in `Cargo.toml`. A patch is regenerated
#    and never edited: it is applied to the crate's source in this tree, both versions go through the
#    same change the move makes to every file, and the patch is the difference, so every hunk header
#    carries the stripped file's line numbers. The patch keeps its prose header and takes `a/`
#    paths from the crate's root. An edit that lands inside a `Name:` block, or that does not apply
#    here, is a finding.
# 5. Unless `--no-build`: writes `Cargo.lock`, runs the host tests and builds each target at the
#    manifest's `rust-version`.
# 5b. Applies every staged patch with `patch -p1 --dry-run -F0` and refuses to finish when one
#    applies at an offset, with fuzz, or not at all (`patch applies exactly`). A hunk header that
#    drifted from its file passes plain `patch` with a note, so the note is what fails.
# 5c. Stages the crate's fuzz targets in cargo-fuzz's layout, under `fuzz/`: a manifest that depends
#    on the crate by `path = ".."`, each target that links this crate and no other crate of the tree,
#    its seeds and the crate's fixture directories that `script/fuzz` adds, in `fuzz/corpus/<target>`,
#    the seed scripts, and a `fuzz/README.md` made of the targets' sections of the notes. The weekly
#    caller passes `fuzz: true` only when the crate has no `unsafe` code (weekly fuzz is opt-in,
#    ruled for crates without it); otherwise it stays `false` and the run says why. Unless
#    `--no-build`, it then builds the targets with `cargo +nightly fuzz build` and runs each once
#    over its corpus (`-runs=0`). The output of a run is withheld on failure, since it holds the
#    input. Each seed script is run in a scratch copy and must rebuild its staged input byte for
#    byte.
# 6. `git init` and `git add --all` in the staged tree, so the checks that read `git ls-files` see
#    what a push would carry. Nothing is committed.
# 7. Runs the template's cheap checks from the pinned revision (standalone, prose, ratchets,
#    naming, orphan modules, links, falsification records) plus two it lacks: citations of this
#    tree, and the word `nife` anywhere in the staged files.
# 8. Prints the nife-side follow-up: each consumer manifest's replacement line, every other
#    tracked file outside `design/` and `notes/` that names the crate, and the removals the fuzz
#    targets' move makes in `fuzz/`, `script/fuzz` and `notes/fuzzing/targets.md`.
#
# It never creates a repository, pushes, tags, publishes or edits this tree. Exit 0 when every check
# passed, 1 when the staged tree has findings (the tree is kept either way), 2 on a usage error.
#
# # EXAMPLES
#
#     $ script/crate-move --dry-run --into "$TMPDIR/fat" --no-build file_allocation_table
#     crate-move: file_allocation_table, a dry run into /tmp/fat
#       ok   template: https://github.com/nifeos/crate-workflows at v7 (ce603f5...)
#       ok   copy: 116 tracked files of crates/file_allocation_table
#       ...
#
# # BUGS
#
# - Kani does not run. `script/crate-standalone` proves the harnesses and replays each patch on
#   this tree's copy; the staged tree's `-p1` patches are checked for form and for applying
#   (`falsify.py --check`), not replayed.
# - The template's heavy jobs (coverage, clippy, rustdoc, supply chain, Miri, mutation) do not run;
#   the repository's first CI run is their first run.
# - There is no live mode. Creating the repository, pushing and tagging are the runbook's steps,
#   taken by hand, because each is a person's or needs one's approval.
set -eu
case " $* " in
*" --selftest "*) exec python3 "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/crate_move.py" --selftest ;;
*" --no-build "*) ;;
*) . "$(dirname "$0")/../helpers/heavy-slot.sh" ;;
esac
ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
exec python3 "$ROOT/helpers/crate_move.py" "$ROOT" "$@"
